# Theory

Stack 'em Up is a card shuffling problem based on a standard deck of 52 playing cards. The deck contains four suits: Clubs, Diamonds, Hearts, and Spades, with 13 cards in each suit.

A shuffle pattern tells how cards move from one position to another. The program reads one or more shuffle patterns and applies them to the deck in the specified order. After all shuffles are completed, the program converts card numbers into card names and prints the final arrangement of the deck.

This problem helps in understanding arrays, indexing, permutations, and simulation techniques used in competitive programming.

---

# Algorithm

1. Initialize the deck from 1 to 52.
2. Read all shuffle patterns.
3. Read the sequence of shuffles to apply.
4. Apply each shuffle to the deck.
5. Update the deck after every shuffle.
6. Convert card numbers into card names.
7. Print the final deck arrangement.

